isabeth Maria", "initials": "EM", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Division of Diagnostic Imaging, Department of Clinical Sciences, Utrecht University, Utrecht, the Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Miles S", "lastName": "Miles", "firstName": "Samantha", "initials": "S", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Division of Diagnostic Imaging, Department of Clinical Sciences, Utrecht University, Utrecht, the Netherlands."}, {"affiliation": "IDEXX Telemedicine Consultants, Department of Radiology, Hoofddorp, the Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Veraa S", "lastName": "Veraa", "firstName": "Stefanie", "initials": "S", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Division of Diagnostic Imaging, Department of Clinical Sciences, Utrecht University, Utrecht, the Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "O'Reilly T", "lastName": "O'Reilly", "firstName": "Thomas", "initials": "T", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "C.J. Gorter MRI Center, Department of Radiology, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, the Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "van den Broek R", "lastName": "van den Broek", "firstName": "Ruben", "initials": "R", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "C.J. Gorter MRI Center, Department of Radiology, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, the Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Najac C", "lastName": "Najac", "firstName": "Chloé", "initials": "C", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "C.J. Gorter MRI Center, Department of Radiology, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, the Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Lena B", "lastName": "Lena", "firstName": "Beatrice", "initials": "B", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "C.J. Gorter MRI Center, Department of Radiology, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, the Netherlands."}]}}, {"fullName": "Webb A", "lastName": "Webb", "firstName": "Andrew", "initials": "A", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "C.J. Gorter MRI Center, Department of Radiology, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, the Netherlands."}]}}]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "1", "volume": "46", "journalIssueId": 4076935, "dateOfPublication": "2026 Dec", "monthOfPublication": 12, "yearOfPublication": 2026, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Vet Q", "title": "The veterinary quarterly", "essn": "1875-5941", "issn": "0165-2176", "nlmid": "7909485", "isoabbreviation": "Vet Q"}, "printPublicationDate": "2026-12-01"}, "pubYear": "2026", "pageInfo": "2720640", "abstractText": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) systems continue to grow in number in veterinary and human medicine with higher field strength magnets increasingly available; however, the associated high costs have created healthcare inequities. This technical feasibility study aimed to evaluate a low-cost, portable, and permanent magnet-based 0.05 T low-field system (LF), originally designed for human neuroimaging, for assessing the canine brain. By using 23 canine cadavers, a scanning protocol was developed, consisting of transverse T1-weighted (T1w) and T2-weighted (T2w) sequences, and compared to a 1.5 T (HF) protocol for the visibility of 22 anatomical features. For the purpose of hydrocephalus evaluation, the LF system showed non-inferior detection of the lateral ventricles (LV) and mesencephalic aqueduct on T2w sequences with >90% conditional probability for detecting these features on the LF, given they were present on the HF. For T1w sequences, the LF system had inconclusive non-inferiority for detection of LV with 76.6% conditional probability. In summary, a canine brain scanning protocol was successfully developed for a 0.05 T MRI with preliminary evaluation suggestive that this device can aid with the detection of hydrocephalus. Future <i>in vivo</i> studies are required to evaluate the device's true clinical application.", "affiliation": "Division of Diagnostic Imaging, Department of Clinical Sciences, Utrecht University, Utrecht, the Netherlands.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["Comparative Study", "research-article", "Evaluation Study", "Journal Article"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "Stichting Bouwstenen voor Dierenwelzijn", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Utrecht Universiteitsfonds", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "European Research Council", "grantId": "101021218", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "K.F. Hein Fonds", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Triodos Foundation", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Dutch Research Council (NWO)", "grantId": "21762", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NWO Open Technologie", "grantId": "21762", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "European Research Council", "acronym": "ERC_", "grantId": "PASMAR 101021218", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "Abri voor Dieren", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Brain", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "DG", "qualifierName": "diagnostic imaging"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Animals"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Dogs"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Hydrocephalus", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "DG", "qualifierName": "diagnostic imaging"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "VE", "qualifierName": "veterinary"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Cadaver"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "descriptorName": "Magnetic Resonance Imaging", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "IS", "qualifierName": "instrumentation"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "VE", "qualifierName": "veterinary"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Feasi